<BaseLayout title="Part III · Periodic Matter and Electronic States" description="How translation symmetry organizes one-particle states into Bloch sectors, reciprocal-space labels, bands, occupations, densities of states, and Fermi surfaces." current="core">
  <nav class="breadcrumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
    <a href={href('/core/')}>Core</a><span aria-hidden="true">/</span><span aria-current="page">Part III</span>
  </nav>

  <p class="secondary">Part III</p>
  <h1>Periodic Matter and Electronic States</h1>
  <p class="lede">A periodic one-particle Hamiltonian is organized by translation symmetry before density-functional theory enters the story. Translation symmetry supplies Bloch states, crystal momentum, bands, and Brillouin-zone equivalence; electron number and occupation then select an electronic state from that spectrum.</p>

  <h2>From molecular orbitals to an extended solid</h2>
  <p>Part II showed why one-particle states can remain useful even when electrons interact: Hartree and Hartree–Fock replace part of the many-electron structure by coupled orbital equations, while retaining clear limits on that reduction. Once one-particle states are useful, the next question is how an extended solid organizes them.</p>
  <p>A finite molecule has a finite spatial extent, so its bound one-particle states can be indexed as a discrete set of orbitals. An ideal crystal adds a different structure: translation by certain vectors reproduces the same physical environment throughout space. A <strong>Bravais lattice</strong> is the set of those vectors,</p>
  <div class="math-display" tabindex="0">
    <math display="block" aria-label="R equals the sum from alpha equals one to d of m alpha a alpha, with integer m alpha">
      <semantics>
        <mrow><mi mathvariant="bold">R</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mo largeop="true">∑</mo><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>d</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><msub><mi mathvariant="bold">a</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mspace width="0.5em"/><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>.</mo></mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf&#123;R&#125;=\sum_&#123;\alpha=1&#125;^d m_\alpha\mathbf&#123;a&#125;_\alpha,\qquad m_\alpha\in\mathbb&#123;Z&#125;.</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>Read this construction from right to left. Each primitive vector <math class="math-inline" aria-label="a alpha"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="bold">a</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf&#123;a&#125;_\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> is taken an integer number <math class="math-inline" aria-label="m alpha"><semantics><msub><mi>m</mi><mi>α</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_\alpha</annotation></semantics></math> of times, and the sum produces every allowed lattice translation <math class="math-inline" aria-label="bold R"><semantics><mi mathvariant="bold">R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf&#123;R&#125;</annotation></semantics></math>. These vectors describe repetitions of the environment, not a list of atomic positions. The atoms still come from the basis attached to each lattice point.</p>
  <p>The lattice is not the crystal. A <strong>basis</strong> or <strong>motif</strong> is the physical content—atoms, ions, or another specified object—attached to each lattice point. The crystal structure is lattice plus basis. Primitive and conventional cells, space groups, and Wyckoff positions refine that description, but the electronic-state argument needs only the translation set and the contents repeated by it.</p>
  <p>Our recurring minimal model is a one-dimensional chain with spacing <math class="math-inline" aria-label="a"><semantics><mi>a</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math>. The general sum then reduces to <math class="math-inline" aria-label="R equals m a"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R=ma</annotation></semantics></math>: one step, two steps, or any integer number of steps along the chain. We will keep returning to this same model so that real-space repetition, Bloch phase, reciprocal periodicity, bands, and filling acquire meaning one layer at a time.</p>
  <p class="equation-source">Source: Martin, <cite>Electronic Structure</cite>, 2nd ed., Ch. 4.</p>

  <h2>Translation symmetry turns repetition into a quantum label</h2>
  <p>Consider the usual one-particle Hamiltonian with a translation-invariant kinetic term and a local potential that repeats under every lattice translation. With no boundary or field that breaks the symmetry,</p>
  <div class="math-display" tabindex="0">
    <math display="block" aria-label="V of r plus R equals V of r, therefore the commutator of H and T sub R is zero">
      <semantics>
        <mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">R</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mo>)</mo><mspace width="1em"/><mo>⇒</mo><mspace width="1em"/><mo>[</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">^</mo></mover><mo>,</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">^</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="bold">R</mi></msub><mo>]</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo></mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">V(\mathbf&#123;r&#125;+\mathbf&#123;R&#125;)=V(\mathbf&#123;r&#125;)\quad\Longrightarrow\quad[\hat&#123;H&#125;,\hat&#123;T&#125;_\mathbf&#123;R&#125;]=0.</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>The first equality says that corresponding points in different cells have the same potential. The commutator says something stronger about the quantum problem: translating a state and then applying the Hamiltonian gives the same result as applying the Hamiltonian first and translating afterward. Consequently, translating an energy eigenstate cannot move it to a different energy. Within any degenerate energy space, the states can therefore be chosen to carry definite translation labels.</p>
  <p>Here we define the translation operator by <math class="math-inline" aria-label="T sub R acting on psi at r equals psi at r plus R"><semantics><mrow><mo>(</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">^</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="bold">R</mi></msub><mi>ψ</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>ψ</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">R</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\hat&#123;T&#125;_\mathbf&#123;R&#125;\psi)(\mathbf&#123;r&#125;)=\psi(\mathbf&#123;r&#125;+\mathbf&#123;R&#125;)</annotation></semantics></math>. Translation is unitary: it changes where the state is evaluated without changing its norm. Its eigenvalue must therefore have magnitude one, so it can only be a phase. Translating by <math class="math-inline" aria-label="R one"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="bold">R</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf&#123;R&#125;_1</annotation></semantics></math> and then <math class="math-inline" aria-label="R two"><semantics><msub><mi mathvariant="bold">R</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf&#123;R&#125;_2</annotation></semantics></math> must also multiply the two phases. The compatible phase family is labelled by a vector <math class="math-inline" aria-label="bold k"><semantics><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf&#123;k&#125;</annotation></semantics></math>. We write <math class="math-inline" aria-label="psi n k"><semantics><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_&#123;n\mathbf&#123;k&#125;&#125;</annotation></semantics></math> because more than one state can share the same translation character; the extra label <math class="math-inline" aria-label="n"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> will become the band index when we solve the energy problem.</p>
  <div class="math-display" tabindex="0">
    <math display="block" aria-label="T sub R psi n k equals exponential i k dot R psi n k, equivalently psi n k at r plus R equals the same phase times psi n k at r">
      <semantics>
        <mtable columnalign="right center left" columnspacing="0.5em" rowspacing="0.55em">
          <mtr><mtd><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy="false">^</mo></mover><mi mathvariant="bold">R</mi></msub><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><mo>=</mo></mtd><mtd><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">e</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">i</mi><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><mo>·</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">R</mi></mrow></msup><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo></mtd></mtr>
          <mtr><mtd><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">R</mi><mo>)</mo></mtd><mtd><mo>=</mo></mtd><mtd><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">e</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">i</mi><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><mo>·</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">R</mi></mrow></msup><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>.</mo></mtd></mtr>
        </mtable>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat&#123;T&#125;_\mathbf&#123;R&#125;\psi_&#123;n\mathbf&#123;k&#125;&#125;=\mathrm&#123;e&#125;^&#123;\mathrm&#123;i&#125;\mathbf&#123;k&#125;\cdot\mathbf&#123;R&#125;&#125;\psi_&#123;n\mathbf&#123;k&#125;&#125;,\qquad \psi_&#123;n\mathbf&#123;k&#125;&#125;(\mathbf&#123;r&#125;+\mathbf&#123;R&#125;)=\mathrm&#123;e&#125;^&#123;\mathrm&#123;i&#125;\mathbf&#123;k&#125;\cdot\mathbf&#123;R&#125;&#125;\psi_&#123;n\mathbf&#123;k&#125;&#125;(\mathbf&#123;r&#125;).</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>Read the second line as a comparison of the same state at equivalent positions in two cells. The magnitude is unchanged because <math class="math-inline" aria-label="the magnitude of exponential i k dot R equals one"><semantics><mrow><mo>|</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">e</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">i</mi><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><mo>·</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">R</mi></mrow></msup><mo>|</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathrm&#123;e&#125;^&#123;\mathrm&#123;i&#125;\mathbf&#123;k&#125;\cdot\mathbf&#123;R&#125;&#125;|=1</annotation></semantics></math>; only the complex phase records how this symmetry sector responds to translation. Here <math class="math-inline" aria-label="i is the imaginary unit"><semantics><mi mathvariant="normal">i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm&#123;i&#125;</annotation></semantics></math> is the imaginary unit, not the orbital or band index used elsewhere. The equation classifies the state—it does not describe a classical electron travelling one cell and accumulating a mechanical phase.</p>
  <div class="translation-views">
    <section>
      <h3>Physical view</h3>
      <p>Moving the coordinate by a lattice vector reaches an equivalent environment. A translation eigenstate reproduces its spatial pattern up to a phase, so the translated state remains in the same symmetry sector.</p>
    </section>
    <section>
      <h3>Mathematical view</h3>
      <p>The commuting translations form an Abelian group. Its one-dimensional unitary representations are the phase factors <math class="math-inline" aria-label="exponential i k dot R"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">e</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">i</mi><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><mo>·</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">R</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm&#123;e&#125;^&#123;\mathrm&#123;i&#125;\mathbf&#123;k&#125;\cdot\mathbf&#123;R&#125;&#125;</annotation></semantics></math>; the vector <math class="math-inline" aria-label="bold k"><semantics><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf&#123;k&#125;</annotation></semantics></math> is the compact label for the entire set of phases.</p>
    </section>
  </div>
  <p>The vector <math class="math-inline" aria-label="bold k"><semantics><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf&#123;k&#125;</annotation></semantics></math> is called <strong>crystal momentum</strong>, but its primary meaning here is this quantum label associated with lattice translations. It is not generally the electron’s free-particle mechanical momentum. What <math class="math-inline" aria-label="bold k"><semantics><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf&#123;k&#125;</annotation></semantics></math> tells us at this stage is how the state transforms, not a unique mechanical velocity or trajectory.</p>

  <h2>Bloch states carry a phase across identical cells</h2>
  <p>The translation equation is Bloch’s theorem in symmetry form. It tells us the phase acquired between equivalent cells, but it does not yet separate that phase from the shape repeated inside each cell. Writing the state as a product makes the two responsibilities visible:</p>
  <div class="math-display" tabindex="0">
    <math display="block" aria-label="psi n k of r equals exponential i k dot r times u n k of r, where u n k is periodic">
      <semantics>
        <mtable columnalign="right center left" columnspacing="0.5em" rowspacing="0.55em">
          <mtr><mtd><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mo>)</mo></mtd><mtd><mo>=</mo></mtd><mtd><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">e</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">i</mi><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><mo>·</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi></mrow></msup><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>,</mo></mtd></mtr>
          <mtr><mtd><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">R</mi><mo>)</mo></mtd><mtd><mo>=</mo></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>.</mo></mtd></mtr>
        </mtable>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_&#123;n\mathbf&#123;k&#125;&#125;(\mathbf&#123;r&#125;)=\mathrm&#123;e&#125;^&#123;\mathrm&#123;i&#125;\mathbf&#123;k&#125;\cdot\mathbf&#123;r&#125;&#125;u_&#123;n\mathbf&#123;k&#125;&#125;(\mathbf&#123;r&#125;),\qquad u_&#123;n\mathbf&#123;k&#125;&#125;(\mathbf&#123;r&#125;+\mathbf&#123;R&#125;)=u_&#123;n\mathbf&#123;k&#125;&#125;(\mathbf&#123;r&#125;).</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>Read the first line as “a plane-wave phase factor times a cell-periodic shape.” The function <math class="math-inline" aria-label="u n k"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_&#123;n\mathbf&#123;k&#125;&#125;</annotation></semantics></math> contains the spatial structure within one cell and repeats exactly under <math class="math-inline" aria-label="bold R"><semantics><mi mathvariant="bold">R</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf&#123;R&#125;</annotation></semantics></math>. The factor <math class="math-inline" aria-label="exponential i k dot r"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">e</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">i</mi><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><mo>·</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm&#123;e&#125;^&#123;\mathrm&#123;i&#125;\mathbf&#123;k&#125;\cdot\mathbf&#123;r&#125;&#125;</annotation></semantics></math> supplies the cell-to-cell phase. Because that factor has unit magnitude, the single-state density <math class="math-inline" aria-label="the magnitude squared of psi n k of r"><semantics><mrow><mo>|</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">r</mi><mo>)</mo><msup><mo>|</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\psi_&#123;n\mathbf&#123;k&#125;&#125;(\mathbf&#123;r&#125;)|^2</annotation></semantics></math> repeats even when its complex amplitude does not.</p>
  <p>A Bloch wave is therefore generally <strong>not periodic</strong> even though the Hamiltonian and crystal are periodic. Only when <math class="math-inline" aria-label="exponential i k dot R equals one"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">e</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">i</mi><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><mo>·</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">R</mi></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm&#123;e&#125;^&#123;\mathrm&#123;i&#125;\mathbf&#123;k&#125;\cdot\mathbf&#123;R&#125;&#125;=1</annotation></semantics></math> for the chosen translation does the full state repeat unchanged. In the chain, one step changes the phase by <math class="math-inline" aria-label="exponential i k a"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">e</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">i</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>a</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm&#123;e&#125;^&#123;\mathrm&#123;i&#125;ka&#125;</annotation></semantics></math>, two steps by <math class="math-inline" aria-label="exponential two i k a"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">e</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant="normal">i</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>a</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm&#123;e&#125;^&#123;2\mathrm&#123;i&#125;ka&#125;</annotation></semantics></math>, while the same <math class="math-inline" aria-label="u k"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_k</annotation></semantics></math> pattern sits inside every cell.</p>

  <figure class="core-diagram bloch-phase" aria-labelledby="bloch-phase-caption">
