    <figcaption id="bloch-phase-caption">For the chain, <math class="math-inline" aria-label="psi k of x plus a equals exponential i k a psi k of x"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">e</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">i</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>a</mi></mrow></msup><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_k(x+a)=\mathrm&#123;e&#125;^&#123;\mathrm&#123;i&#125;ka&#125;\psi_k(x)</annotation></semantics></math>. The local pattern repeats, while its complex phase advances from cell to cell.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <div class="concept-check" role="note" aria-labelledby="part-iii-check-one">
    <h3 id="part-iii-check-one">Check your understanding</h3>
    <p><strong>Question.</strong> If the crystal is periodic, why is a Bloch wave not generally periodic?</p>
    <p><strong>Answer.</strong> Translation symmetry requires the full state <math class="math-inline" aria-label="psi n k"><semantics><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\psi_&#123;n\mathbf&#123;k&#125;&#125;</annotation></semantics></math> to return up to the phase <math class="math-inline" aria-label="exponential i k dot R"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">e</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">i</mi><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><mo>·</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">R</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm&#123;e&#125;^&#123;\mathrm&#123;i&#125;\mathbf&#123;k&#125;\cdot\mathbf&#123;R&#125;&#125;</annotation></semantics></math>, not necessarily to return unchanged. The factor <math class="math-inline" aria-label="u n k"><semantics><msub><mi>u</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">u_&#123;n\mathbf&#123;k&#125;&#125;</annotation></semantics></math> is periodic; the complete Bloch state is periodic only when the translation phase equals one.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>Reciprocal space removes redundant translation labels</h2>
  <p>The phase label now creates its own question: are all vectors <math class="math-inline" aria-label="bold k"><semantics><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf&#123;k&#125;</annotation></semantics></math> distinct translation characters? To answer it, shift <math class="math-inline" aria-label="bold k"><semantics><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf&#123;k&#125;</annotation></semantics></math> by a vector <math class="math-inline" aria-label="bold G"><semantics><mi mathvariant="bold">G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf&#123;G&#125;</annotation></semantics></math> and ask what condition leaves the phase for <em>every</em> real-space lattice translation unchanged:</p>
  <div class="math-display" tabindex="0">
    <math display="block" aria-label="Exponential i k plus G dot R equals exponential i k dot R when exponential i G dot R equals one, equivalently G dot R equals two pi m">
      <semantics>
        <mtable columnalign="right center left" columnspacing="0.5em" rowspacing="0.55em">
          <mtr><mtd><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">e</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">i</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">G</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>·</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">R</mi></mrow></msup></mtd><mtd><mo>=</mo></mtd><mtd><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">e</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">i</mi><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><mo>·</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">R</mi></mrow></msup><mspace width="0.7em"/><mtext>if</mtext><mspace width="0.5em"/><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">e</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">i</mi><mi mathvariant="bold">G</mi><mo>·</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">R</mi></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo></mtd></mtr>
          <mtr><mtd><mi mathvariant="bold">G</mi><mo>·</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">R</mi></mtd><mtd><mo>=</mo></mtd><mtd><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>m</mi><mo>,</mo><mspace width="0.5em"/><mi>m</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>.</mo></mtd></mtr>
        </mtable>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm&#123;e&#125;^&#123;\mathrm&#123;i&#125;(\mathbf&#123;k&#125;+\mathbf&#123;G&#125;)\cdot\mathbf&#123;R&#125;&#125;=\mathrm&#123;e&#125;^&#123;\mathrm&#123;i&#125;\mathbf&#123;k&#125;\cdot\mathbf&#123;R&#125;&#125;\ \text&#123;if&#125;\ \mathrm&#123;e&#125;^&#123;\mathrm&#123;i&#125;\mathbf&#123;G&#125;\cdot\mathbf&#123;R&#125;&#125;=1,\qquad \mathbf&#123;G&#125;\cdot\mathbf&#123;R&#125;=2\pi m.</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>The first line factors the shifted phase into the old phase and an extra factor <math class="math-inline" aria-label="exponential i G dot R"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">e</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">i</mi><mi mathvariant="bold">G</mi><mo>·</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">R</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm&#123;e&#125;^&#123;\mathrm&#123;i&#125;\mathbf&#123;G&#125;\cdot\mathbf&#123;R&#125;&#125;</annotation></semantics></math>. Requiring that extra factor to equal one for every lattice vector gives the second line. The vectors satisfying it form the <strong>reciprocal lattice</strong>. Thus reciprocal space is not introduced as a computational trick: it is the natural space in which the translation characters repeat.</p>
  <p>For the chain, <math class="math-inline" aria-label="R equals m a"><semantics><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R=ma</annotation></semantics></math>. Shifting <math class="math-inline" aria-label="k"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> by <math class="math-inline" aria-label="two pi n over a"><semantics><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow><mi>a</mi></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\pi n/a</annotation></semantics></math> multiplies the phase by <math class="math-inline" aria-label="exponential i two pi n m equals one"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">e</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">i</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi><mi>n</mi><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm&#123;e&#125;^&#123;\mathrm&#123;i&#125;2\pi nm&#125;=1</annotation></semantics></math> for every integer cell <math class="math-inline" aria-label="m"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>. That concrete phase test is why <math class="math-inline" aria-label="k"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> and <math class="math-inline" aria-label="k plus G"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k+G</annotation></semantics></math> are redundant labels.</p>
  <p>The <strong>first Brillouin zone</strong> is the conventional choice: the Wigner–Seitz cell of the reciprocal lattice. This geometric definition follows the physical reason for choosing it—crystal momentum repeats modulo reciprocal-lattice vectors. For the chain,</p>
  <div class="math-display" tabindex="0">
    <math display="block" aria-label="G equals two pi n over a, and the first Brillouin zone is minus pi over a less than or equal to k less than pi over a">
      <semantics>
        <mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi></mrow><mi>a</mi></mfrac><mi>n</mi><mo>,</mo><mspace width="0.4em"/><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>;</mo><mspace width="1.2em"/><mo>−</mo><mfrac><mi>π</mi><mi>a</mi></mfrac><mo>≤</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mfrac><mi>π</mi><mi>a</mi></mfrac><mo>.</mo></mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G=\frac&#123;2\pi&#125;&#123;a&#125;n,\ n\in\mathbb&#123;Z&#125;;\qquad -\frac&#123;\pi&#125;&#123;a&#125;\le k&lt;\frac&#123;\pi&#125;&#123;a&#125;.</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>The half-open interval contains one representative of every translation character without counting the two equivalent zone edges twice. In more dimensions the same nonredundancy requirement selects one primitive reciprocal cell; the first Brillouin zone is its Wigner–Seitz choice.</p>
  <figure class="core-diagram lattice-duality" aria-labelledby="lattice-duality-caption">
    <svg viewBox="0 0 360 226" role="img" aria-labelledby="lattice-duality-title lattice-duality-desc">
      <title id="lattice-duality-title">Real and reciprocal lattices of a one-dimensional chain</title>
      <desc id="lattice-duality-desc">Equally spaced real-space sites separated by a are paired with reciprocal-lattice marks separated by two pi over a. The first Brillouin zone extends from minus pi over a to pi over a.</desc>
      <text x="14" y="22" class="diagram-heading">Real space</text>
      <line x1="36" y1="68" x2="324" y2="68" class="diagram-axis"/>
      <g class="diagram-sites">
        <circle cx="60" cy="68" r="5"/><circle cx="120" cy="68" r="5"/><circle cx="180" cy="68" r="5"/><circle cx="240" cy="68" r="5"/><circle cx="300" cy="68" r="5"/>
      </g>
      <line x1="120" y1="92" x2="180" y2="92" class="diagram-measure"/>
      <line x1="120" y1="87" x2="120" y2="97" class="diagram-measure"/><line x1="180" y1="87" x2="180" y2="97" class="diagram-measure"/>
      <text x="147" y="110">a</text>
      <text x="14" y="139" class="diagram-heading">Reciprocal space</text>
      <rect x="150" y="151" width="60" height="42" class="diagram-zone"/>
      <line x1="36" y1="172" x2="324" y2="172" class="diagram-axis"/>
      <g class="diagram-ticks">
        <line x1="60" y1="162" x2="60" y2="182"/><line x1="120" y1="162" x2="120" y2="182"/><line x1="180" y1="159" x2="180" y2="185"/><line x1="240" y1="162" x2="240" y2="182"/><line x1="300" y1="162" x2="300" y2="182"/>
      </g>
      <text x="51" y="203">−2G₀</text><text x="114" y="203">−G₀</text><text x="176" y="203">0</text><text x="236" y="203">G₀</text><text x="293" y="203">2G₀</text>
      <text x="137" y="220">first BZ: [−π/a, π/a)</text>
    </svg>
    <figcaption id="lattice-duality-caption">For the one-dimensional chain, <math class="math-inline" aria-label="G sub zero equals two pi over a"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>π</mi></mrow><mi>a</mi></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G_0=2\pi/a</annotation></semantics></math>. Reciprocal space is not added as a numerical trick; it records which translation phases are distinct.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <dl class="paired-definitions">
    <dt><math class="math-inline" aria-label="bold G"><semantics><mi mathvariant="bold">G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf&#123;G&#125;</annotation></semantics></math> — reciprocal-lattice vector</dt>
    <dd>A discrete vector defined by the real lattice; adding it does not change a translation character.</dd>
    <dt><math class="math-inline" aria-label="bold k"><semantics><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf&#123;k&#125;</annotation></semantics></math> — Bloch-state label</dt>
    <dd>A generally continuous label within one Brillouin zone that identifies a translation-symmetry sector.</dd>
  </dl>
  <p>Both live in reciprocal space, but they are not the same object. The combination <math class="math-inline" aria-label="bold k plus bold G"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><mo>+</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf&#123;k&#125;+\mathbf&#123;G&#125;</annotation></semantics></math> contains a Bloch-state label and a reciprocal-lattice vector; it does not make the two objects interchangeable.</p>

  <h2>At every k there is a family of eigenstates</h2>
  <p>Choosing a translation sector does not finish the eigenvalue problem. It fixes how a state changes between cells, but many different spatial patterns, orbital characters, or internal nodes can share the same phase label. The Hamiltonian must still be diagonalized inside each <math class="math-inline" aria-label="bold k"><semantics><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf&#123;k&#125;</annotation></semantics></math> sector:</p>
  <div class="math-display" tabindex="0">
    <math display="block" aria-label="H psi n k equals E n of k psi n k">
      <semantics>
        <mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">^</mo></mover><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><mo>)</mo><msub><mi>ψ</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>.</mo></mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat&#123;H&#125;\psi_&#123;n\mathbf&#123;k&#125;&#125;=E_n(\mathbf&#123;k&#125;)\psi_&#123;n\mathbf&#123;k&#125;&#125;.</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>On the left, the Hamiltonian acts on a state already assigned to sector <math class="math-inline" aria-label="bold k"><semantics><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf&#123;k&#125;</annotation></semantics></math>. On the right, the same state returns multiplied by its energy. The label <math class="math-inline" aria-label="n"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> distinguishes the different eigenstates available inside that sector; it is the <strong>band index</strong>. Repeating the eigenvalue problem as <math class="math-inline" aria-label="bold k"><semantics><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf&#123;k&#125;</annotation></semantics></math> varies produces energy branches <math class="math-inline" aria-label="E n of bold k"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_n(\mathbf&#123;k&#125;)</annotation></semantics></math>: the bands.</p>
  <p>Changing <math class="math-inline" aria-label="bold k"><semantics><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf&#123;k&#125;</annotation></semantics></math> changes the relative phase between repeated cells, and therefore changes how amplitudes from different cells combine. That is the physical reason the energy can disperse with <math class="math-inline" aria-label="bold k"><semantics><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf&#123;k&#125;</annotation></semantics></math>. Away from degeneracies one may follow an individual branch continuously; at a degeneracy, assigning separate band labels can be conventional. Additional point and space symmetries can enforce degeneracies and constrain couplings at special points or lines, but translation creates the organizing <math class="math-inline" aria-label="bold k"><semantics><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf&#123;k&#125;</annotation></semantics></math> sectors before any density-functional construction is considered.</p>
  <p>The one-orbital chain shows how a band can grow from repeated local states. Begin with one orbital of energy <math class="math-inline" aria-label="epsilon zero"><semantics><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon_0</annotation></semantics></math> on each site and retain only a real nearest-neighbor matrix element <math class="math-inline" aria-label="t"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>. With the convention that this off-diagonal Hamiltonian element is <math class="math-inline" aria-label="t"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>, the single band is</p>
  <div class="math-display" tabindex="0">
    <math display="block" aria-label="E of k equals epsilon zero plus two t cosine k a">
      <semantics>
        <mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>t</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">cos</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>k</mi><mi>a</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>.</mo></mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(k)=\varepsilon_0+2t\cos(ka).</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>Each symbol carries a different piece of the model. The local energy <math class="math-inline" aria-label="epsilon zero"><semantics><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varepsilon_0</annotation></semantics></math> is what the repeated orbital would have without intersite coupling. The matrix element <math class="math-inline" aria-label="t"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> measures coupling to either nearest neighbor. A Bloch state combines the right neighbor with phase <math class="math-inline" aria-label="exponential i k a"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">e</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">i</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>a</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm&#123;e&#125;^&#123;\mathrm&#123;i&#125;ka&#125;</annotation></semantics></math> and the left neighbor with <math class="math-inline" aria-label="exponential minus i k a"><semantics><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">i</mi><mi>k</mi><mi>a</mi></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm&#123;e&#125;^&#123;-\mathrm&#123;i&#125;ka&#125;</annotation></semantics></math>; their sum is <math class="math-inline" aria-label="two cosine k a"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant="normal">cos</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>k</mi><mi>a</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\cos(ka)</annotation></semantics></math>. The cosine is therefore the interference of equal translated couplings, not an assumed universal band shape.</p>
  <p>The limiting case makes the role of coupling explicit:</p>
  <div class="math-display" tabindex="0">
    <math display="block" aria-label="As t approaches zero, E of k approaches epsilon zero">
      <semantics>
        <mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="true">lim</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></munder><mspace width="0.25em"/><mi>E</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>ε</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>.</mo></mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lim_&#123;t\to 0&#125;E(k)=\varepsilon_0.</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>When <math class="math-inline" aria-label="t"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> vanishes, different sites no longer communicate, every <math class="math-inline" aria-label="k"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> value has the same energy, and the band collapses back to the repeated local level. Increasing <math class="math-inline" aria-label="the magnitude of t"><semantics><mrow><mo>|</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|t|</annotation></semantics></math> broadens the band. For the stated sign convention, <math class="math-inline" aria-label="t less than zero"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t&lt;0</annotation></semantics></math> places its minimum at <math class="math-inline" aria-label="k equals zero"><semantics><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k=0</annotation></semantics></math>; changing the sign reverses the ordering.</p>
  <p>A ring of <math class="math-inline" aria-label="N"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> sites has <math class="math-inline" aria-label="N"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> allowed <math class="math-inline" aria-label="k"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> values in this band. They become dense as the periodic chain grows, turning the finite set of split levels into a continuous-looking branch. Real materials may have many orbitals, longer-range couplings, and non-cosine dispersions; the general definition remains the eigenvalue spectrum of the declared periodic one-particle Hamiltonian.</p>

  <figure class="core-diagram band-formation" aria-labelledby="band-formation-caption">
    <svg viewBox="0 0 360 214" role="img" aria-labelledby="band-formation-title band-formation-desc">
      <title id="band-formation-title">A repeated atomic level becomes a dispersive band</title>
      <desc id="band-formation-desc">One isolated energy level appears on the left, several split levels for a short chain appear in the center, and a continuous cosine-like energy band across the Brillouin zone appears on the right.</desc>
      <text x="30" y="24" class="diagram-heading">1 site</text><text x="139" y="24" class="diagram-heading">few sites</text><text x="256" y="24" class="diagram-heading">periodic chain</text>
      <line x1="22" y1="178" x2="92" y2="178" class="diagram-axis"/><line x1="22" y1="178" x2="22" y2="46" class="diagram-axis"/>
      <line x1="38" y1="108" x2="78" y2="108" class="diagram-level"/><text x="47" y="98">ε₀</text>
      <line x1="126" y1="178" x2="205" y2="178" class="diagram-axis"/><line x1="126" y1="178" x2="126" y2="46" class="diagram-axis"/>
      <g class="diagram-levels"><line x1="141" y1="79" x2="190" y2="79"/><line x1="141" y1="94" x2="190" y2="94"/><line x1="141" y1="110" x2="190" y2="110"/><line x1="141" y1="127" x2="190" y2="127"/><line x1="141" y1="143" x2="190" y2="143"/></g>
      <line x1="232" y1="178" x2="342" y2="178" class="diagram-axis"/><line x1="232" y1="178" x2="232" y2="46" class="diagram-axis"/>
      <path d="M242 74 C268 74, 273 145, 287 145 C301 145, 306 74, 332 74" class="diagram-band"/>
      <text x="237" y="198">−π/a</text><text x="284" y="198">0</text><text x="316" y="198">π/a</text>
      <text x="8" y="43">E</text><text x="112" y="43">E</text><text x="218" y="43">E</text><text x="336" y="181">k</text>
    </svg>
    <figcaption id="band-formation-caption">Equivalent local levels split when sites couple. Translation symmetry labels the resulting states by <math class="math-inline" aria-label="k"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math>; in the extended limit their energies trace a band.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>Occupation turns a spectrum into an electronic state</h2>
  <p>A list of bands is still only a list of available states. The electron number and occupation rule decide which <math class="math-inline" aria-label="n bold k"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\mathbf&#123;k&#125;</annotation></semantics></math> states are actually filled. At zero temperature in the simplest independent-particle picture, one orders the states by energy and fills them up to an occupation boundary called the Fermi level. A spin-independent model may place two opposite-spin electrons in each spatial Bloch state, but that factor of two is an assumption, not a universal counting rule.</p>
  <p>If all bands below the occupation boundary are completely filled and separated from empty bands by a gap, the one-particle filling is insulating. If a band is only partially filled or crosses the Fermi level, the one-particle spectrum is metallic. A zero-gap touching case requires separate classification, and in a gapped system occupation alone does not fix the zero-temperature chemical potential to a unique point inside the gap. This is a band classification of a declared one-particle Hamiltonian. Strong interactions or symmetry breaking can produce insulating behavior that the simplest filling picture does not explain.</p>
  <p>The chain makes the distinction concrete. A partially filled cosine band has occupied states around its minimum and empty states closer to its maximum. Their boundary consists of Fermi points—typically <math class="math-inline" aria-label="plus or minus k F"><semantics><mrow><mo>±</mo><msub><mi>k</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">F</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm k_\mathrm&#123;F&#125;</annotation></semantics></math> for the symmetric one-dimensional model. Filling every allowed state removes those Fermi points, although calling the result insulating also assumes a gap to any higher band omitted from the one-orbital model.</p>
  <p>For a metal, the reciprocal-space occupation boundary satisfies</p>
  <div class="math-display" tabindex="0">
    <math display="block" aria-label="E n of bold k equals E sub F">
      <semantics>
        <mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">F</mi></msub><mo>.</mo></mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_n(\mathbf&#123;k&#125;)=E_\mathrm&#123;F&#125;.</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>This equation is not another eigenvalue problem. It selects, from all existing band states, those exactly at the occupation boundary. The solutions form the <strong>Fermi surface</strong> in three-dimensional <math class="math-inline" aria-label="k"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> space, a Fermi contour in two dimensions, or Fermi points in the chain. It is not a physical crystal surface; it is the locus separating occupied and unoccupied one-particle states at the Fermi energy under the stated zero-temperature picture.</p>

  <h2>Band structure and density of states reorganize the same spectrum</h2>
  <p>A band structure retains where each eigenvalue occurs: it shows energy as a function of band index and crystal momentum. That is essential when direction, valley, crossing, or dispersion matters, but it is awkward for a different question: how much state weight is available near a chosen energy, regardless of where those states occur? The density of states, or DOS, deliberately forgets the momentum location and counts over the whole Brillouin zone. A normalized conceptual form in <math class="math-inline" aria-label="d"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math> dimensions is</p>
  <div class="math-display" tabindex="0">
    <math display="block" aria-label="D of E equals sum n integral over the Brillouin zone d to the d k over Omega B Z times delta of E minus E n of k">
      <semantics>
        <mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>=</mo><msub><mo largeop="true">∑</mo><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mo largeop="true">∫</mo><mtext>BZ</mtext></msub><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">d</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi></mrow><msub><mi>Ω</mi><mtext>BZ</mtext></msub></mfrac><mi>δ</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>E</mi><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><mo>)</mo><mo>)</mo><mo>.</mo></mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D(E)=\sum_n\int_\mathrm&#123;BZ&#125;\frac&#123;\mathrm&#123;d&#125;^d\mathbf&#123;k&#125;&#125;&#123;\Omega_\mathrm&#123;BZ&#125;&#125;\,\delta(E-E_n(\mathbf&#123;k&#125;)).</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>Read the formula from inside outward. For a particular band <math class="math-inline" aria-label="n"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> and label <math class="math-inline" aria-label="bold k"><semantics><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf&#123;k&#125;</annotation></semantics></math>, the delta factor contributes only when the band energy <math class="math-inline" aria-label="E n of k"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>E</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E_n(\mathbf&#123;k&#125;)</annotation></semantics></math> matches the energy being counted. The Brillouin-zone integral visits every nonredundant <math class="math-inline" aria-label="bold k"><semantics><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf&#123;k&#125;</annotation></semantics></math> label, and the sum repeats that count for every band. Dividing by <math class="math-inline" aria-label="Omega B Z"><semantics><msub><mi>Ω</mi><mtext>BZ</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Omega_\mathrm&#123;BZ&#125;</annotation></semantics></math> fixes the stated per-cell normalization.</p>
  <p>With this convention, each state family counted by <math class="math-inline" aria-label="n"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> contributes one state per primitive cell when the DOS is integrated over energy; spin, internal-state, and per-volume conventions add their declared factors. In the chain, a slowly varying part of <math class="math-inline" aria-label="E of k"><semantics><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>(</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E(k)</annotation></semantics></math> places many nearby <math class="math-inline" aria-label="k"><semantics><mi>k</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">k</annotation></semantics></math> states into a narrow energy interval, so it contributes strongly to the DOS. A DOS peak therefore says “many states near this energy,” not “the band is high here,” and it cannot reveal which momentum region supplied those states.</p>

  <figure class="core-diagram band-dos" aria-labelledby="band-dos-caption">
    <svg viewBox="0 0 360 230" role="img" aria-labelledby="band-dos-title band-dos-desc">
      <title id="band-dos-title">Band dispersion and density of states are different views</title>
      <desc id="band-dos-desc">On the left, two energy bands vary with crystal momentum. On the right, a density-of-states curve counts states at each energy without preserving their momentum locations. A shared Fermi-level line crosses both views.</desc>
      <text x="75" y="22" class="diagram-heading">Eₙ(k)</text><text x="267" y="22" class="diagram-heading">D(E)</text>
      <line x1="26" y1="198" x2="190" y2="198" class="diagram-axis"/><line x1="26" y1="198" x2="26" y2="38" class="diagram-axis"/>
