  <h2>A band path is not the Brillouin zone</h2>
  <p>Symmetry identifies special points and lines where degeneracies or selection constraints may be especially informative. Connecting those selected points produces a useful band plot because the eye can follow energy branches along a one-dimensional route. But in a two- or three-dimensional crystal, that route omits almost every other point in the reciprocal cell.</p>
  <p>An average or count that depends on all states needs the full zone. For a quantity <math class="math-inline" aria-label="A of bold k"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><mo>)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A(\mathbf&#123;k&#125;)</annotation></semantics></math>, the continuum object has the distinct form</p>
  <div class="math-display" tabindex="0">
    <math display="block" aria-label="Average A equals one over Omega B Z integral over the Brillouin zone A of k d to the d k">
      <semantics>
        <mrow><mo>⟨</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>⟩</mo><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>Ω</mi><mtext>BZ</mtext></msub></mfrac><msub><mo largeop="true">∫</mo><mtext>BZ</mtext></msub><mi>A</mi><mo>(</mo><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><mo>)</mo><mspace width="0.18em"/><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">d</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi><mo>.</mo></mrow>
        <annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle A\rangle=\frac&#123;1&#125;&#123;\Omega_\mathrm&#123;BZ&#125;&#125;\int_\mathrm&#123;BZ&#125;A(\mathbf&#123;k&#125;)\,\mathrm&#123;d&#125;^d\mathbf&#123;k&#125;.</annotation>
      </semantics>
    </math>
  </div>
  <p>The volume element <math class="math-inline" aria-label="d to the d k"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi mathvariant="normal">d</mi><mi>d</mi></msup><mi mathvariant="bold">k</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm&#123;d&#125;^d\mathbf&#123;k&#125;</annotation></semantics></math> says to include the entire <math class="math-inline" aria-label="d dimensional"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional zone; the factor <math class="math-inline" aria-label="one over Omega B Z"><semantics><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>Ω</mi><mtext>BZ</mtext></msub></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1/\Omega_\mathrm&#123;BZ&#125;</annotation></semantics></math> makes this an average rather than a total. A high-symmetry path supplies neither the full domain nor its weights, so it cannot generally evaluate the integral. For the same reason, it cannot prove that no off-path band extremum or Fermi pocket exists.</p>
  <p>Part V will explain how full-zone objects are represented by finite weighted sampling; DFT Research Workflow owns observable-specific convergence. Here the conceptual boundary is enough: a visualization path, an integration mesh, and a full-zone search answer different questions.</p>
  <p class="repair-links">For repair depth, see <a href={href('/theory/crystallography/')}>Crystallography</a> for cells and reciprocal geometry, <a href={href('/theory/fourier-analysis/')}>Fourier Analysis</a> for periodic representations, <a href={href('/theory/group-theory-and-symmetry/')}>Group Theory and Symmetry</a> for degeneracies and selection rules, or <a href={href('/theory/solid-state-physics/')}>Solid-State Physics</a> for broader condensed-matter context. <a href={href('/theory/brillouin-zone-sampling/')}>Brillouin-Zone Sampling</a> develops the later quadrature problem without turning a band path into an integration domain.</p>

