  <p>We now know that if a one-particle Hamiltonian is periodic, its eigenstates can be chosen as Bloch states and its eigenvalues form bands whose occupation defines a one-particle electronic state. But the real electrons still interact, and Part II showed the limits of an orbital mean field. Can the interacting ground-state problem be reformulated without representing the full many-electron wavefunction?</p>
  <p>Part IV will ask how a density-based theory can make that reformulation while its auxiliary periodic one-particle Hamiltonian inherits the structure established here.</p>

